// The attachment kinds: how two parts of a structure hold together where they touch, or a trunk to
// the ground, in the game's environment destruction (game state only). One shared list, beside
// `materials.ron`: a construction's spec names a kind for each pair of parts it joins
// (`tools/constructions/<id>.house.ron`, its `joins`), the bake writes those names into the construction
// (`assets/constructions/<id>/<id>.construction.ron`, its `attachments`), and the game reads each name here
// at load, refusing one this list does not hold. A tree species names the root plate its trunk
// holds to the ground by (`assets/trees/wood.ron`, its row's `anchor`). Inside one component its
// chunks hold by the component's own seam, never by a kind here: a trunk's segments hold by the
// wood's own along-grain seam (`wood.ron`).
//
// No fastener is modelled: a kind is a seam spread over the joint's whole contact area, never a
// count of nails or bolts (`.agents/docs/design/construction-destruction.md`, "Attachment kinds").
//
// Every kind is one of four:
// - `Seam("<substance>")`: the seam of that row of `materials.ron` (its bulk `tensile` and
//   `fracture_energy`), the numbers' one source.
// - `Stated(tensile_pa, fracture_energy_j_per_m2)`: a seam this list states: its tensile strength
//   across the joint's area, Pa, and its mode-I fracture energy, J/m², each authored
//   `(value, Kind("source or reason"))` (`src/provenance.rs`). A kind missing either does not load.
// - `Resting`: one part laid on the other with nothing fixing it: no pull strength and no fracture
//   work, held by its weight and the friction of the two materials alone.
// - `RootPlate(overturning_nm_per_kg)`: a trunk's root plate in its soil, joining the trunk to the
//   ground and no two parts: its critical overturning moment per kilogram of FRESH STEM MASS,
//   N·m/kg, multiplied by a mass in kg with no extra g, authored `(value, Kind("source"))`. An
//   ultimate moment only. ASSUMED rigid-plastic release with zero separation work: the source
//   supplies no work measurement, so a blow pays only the lift over the hinge. This is not a
//   measured zero root-separation energy. Each row binds its species, soil and rooting depth.
//
// Every root plate here describes unrotted trees on freely draining mineral soil, rooting depth
// 40-80 cm (MEASURED domain of Nicoll's A2 class). Applying that site profile to the game's trees
// is ASSUMED; it is no coefficient for every soil, and another soil or rooting depth is a kind of
// its own. The moment includes the applied pull and the overhanging self-weight.
//
// Bolted and welded join the list once a construction or a vehicle uses them.
{
    // A mortar bed, as between a masonry's own courses: a wall coursed into the next at a corner, a
    // timber bedded on a wall (ASSUMED: a mortar bed holds a timber as it holds the next course).
    // Brick is the one masonry a construction is of.
    // Its c and G_II are also Brick's bulk values, sourced there once.
    "mortared": Seam("Brick"),
    // Timber nailed to timber or to a wall plate, as rafters to their plate and to each other at a
    // ridge: the nails per square metre of contact times one nail's withdrawal capacity, and its
    // withdrawal work. One nailing density stands for every nailed joint: a joint that plays as
    // nailed far more densely (a ridge's two nails per 0.6 m over its 0.0625 m mitred face, 53.3 per
    // m², DERIVED) is a kind of its own once play shows it needs one.
    // Shear cohesion c and mode-II separation work G_II are unsourced for this reference
    // nailing. The material source audit is recorded in
    // .agents/scratch/collision-c6-shear-sources.md. A stated kind holds no shear pair, so its
    // seams answer by the opening law alone and no punch ring forms across them.
    "nailed": Stated(
        tensile_pa: (3240.0, Derived("one nail's withdrawal capacity × 10.7 nails per m² of
            contact. One nail: CLAIMED, not read (EN 1995-1-1:2004 §8.3.2, eqs. 8.24 and 8.25,
            recalled): a smooth nail's characteristic withdrawal strength f_ax = 20e-6 ρ_k² N/mm²,
            ρ_k 350 kg/m³ for C24 spruce (EN 338, recalled), 2.45 N/mm², times its diameter and its
            point's penetration; ASSUMED a 3.1 × 75 mm wire nail 40 mm into the timber it holds,
            so 304 N. The density: ASSUMED two nails per 0.6 m of joint (rafters at 600 mm
            centres, each skew-nailed to its plate with two) over a 0.3125 m contact strip (a
            0.25 m wall top under a 3 : 4 slope, over its cosine 0.8): 10.7 per m², 3.24 kPa")),
        fracture_energy_j_per_m2: (65.0, Derived("one nail's withdrawal work × 10.7 nails per m²
            of contact (the density `tensile_pa` states): ASSUMED, its withdrawal force falls
            linearly from its 304 N peak to nothing over its 40 mm penetration, so 6.1 J a nail,
            and 65 J/m²")),
    ),
    // Laid on the other part, nothing fixing it: a roof plane on a gable's rake.
    "resting": Resting,
    // A Norway spruce's root plate on freely draining mineral soil, rooting depth 40-80 cm.
    "norway_spruce_root_plate": RootPlate(
        overturning_nm_per_kg: (154.6, Measured("MEASURED transcription of a field-data
            regression: Nicoll (2006), The effects of soil, terrain and wind climate on tree root
            system development and anchorage, Table 2-6 (printed p. 38), row NS, soil A, root
            class 2: Creg 154.6 N m/kg fresh stem mass; 22 trees at 2 sites, s.e. 19.2 N m/kg.
            Species codes in Table 2-3, soil/depth in Table 2-1: Norway spruce, freely draining
            mineral soil, medium rooting depth 40-80 cm. Rot and snapped trees excluded. Moment
            includes applied pull and overhanging self-weight; multiply by fresh stem mass in kg,
            not weight in N. Transferred site profile ASSUMED for the game, not universal soil
            anchorage. https://era.ed.ac.uk/server/api/core/bitstreams/bf65a702-ade0-4a94-8a60-e1eaf7e8b79c/content")),
    ),
    // A Scots pine's root plate on freely draining mineral soil, rooting depth 40-80 cm.
    "scots_pine_root_plate": RootPlate(
        overturning_nm_per_kg: (146.4, Measured("MEASURED transcription of a field-data
            regression: Nicoll (2006), Table 2-6 (printed p. 38), row SP, soil A, root class 2:
            Creg 146.4 N m/kg fresh stem mass; 71 trees at 5 sites, s.e. 9.6 N m/kg. Tables 2-3
            and 2-1 bind Scots pine, freely draining mineral soil and rooting depth 40-80 cm. Rot
            and snapped trees excluded. Total applied-plus-self-weight moment; mass in kg, no
            extra g. Transferred site profile ASSUMED for the game. Same Nicoll thesis URL as
            norway_spruce_root_plate above.")),
    ),
    // A silver birch's root plate on freely draining mineral soil, rooting depth 40-80 cm: the
    // Scots pine's, transferred.
    "silver_birch_root_plate": RootPlate(
        overturning_nm_per_kg: (146.4, Unknown("ASSUMED Scots-pine analogue for silver birch: Nicoll
            (2006), Table 2-6 SP/A2 coefficient 146.4 N m/kg fresh stem mass, on freely draining
            mineral soil with rooting depth 40-80 cm. The table has no birch row; no matched
            silver-birch fresh-stem-mass regression was read. This transfer is not a measured birch
            anchorage or a bound on it. Site profile also ASSUMED for the game; kg fresh stem mass,
            no extra g. Same Nicoll thesis URL as norway_spruce_root_plate above. Settled by a
            matched silver-birch root-plate moment versus fresh-stem-mass regression")),
    ),
}
